-- **`bibliothek/linux-kmod` -- the Linux kernel primitives a Gabbro module
-- binds, as ordinary user code** (server lane, TODO section 0e K7, 2026-09-28).
--
-- WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT. Simon's binding constraint
-- (2026-09-27) is *"API calls are always user-made"*: a Gabbro program reaches
-- an operating system only through items IT declares, with their ABI, their
-- effects, their costs and the assumption their bodies keep. Nothing about
-- Linux is baked into the checker, the emitter or the runtime.
--
-- Until 2026-09-28 the module RUNTIME was the one exception, and it was
-- measured rather than argued away (`dokumente/OFFEN.md` O35): `kmodul.c`
-- called `kthread_run`, `wait_for_completion`, `complete` and `pr_err`,
-- `arena.c` called `vzalloc`, `vfree` and `module_param`, and `sperre.h`
-- expanded to `raw_spin_lock`, `raw_spin_lock_irqsave` and `smp_processor_id`
-- -- twelve kernel names no program had ever declared. **This file is where
-- those twelve now stand**, and it is a FILE OF A PROGRAM: a manifest names it
-- beside the program's own sources, the checker reads it like any other unit,
-- and the C bodies next door (`linux-kmod.c`) are compiled into the module as
-- the program's own foreign bodies. *It is a library in the sense a header file
-- is one -- something you copy into your build and may edit -- and not in the
-- sense a compiler built-in is one.*
--
-- **A different kernel gets a different file, not a different Gabbro.** That is
-- the whole point of the arrangement, and it is why the twelve names below are
-- the interface the generated module driver calls and the kernel's own
-- names appear only in `linux-kmod.c`. A port to a BSD, to a different Linux
-- version whose exported surface differs, or to a kernel that has no
-- `vmalloc`, is a new pair of files here and nothing else.
--
-- WHAT A PROGRAM DOES WITH IT. Three lines in the manifest of a `module` unit:
--
--     unit driver module laden entladen
--       my-driver.gab
--       my-driver.c                          -- the program's own kernel calls
--       bibliothek/linux-kmod/linux-kmod.gab -- these declarations
--       bibliothek/linux-kmod/linux-kmod.c   -- their bodies
--       bibliothek/linux-kmod/stdatomic.h    -- only if it declares an `atomic`
--
-- and `gabbro build` REFUSES a `module` unit that uses a lock, an arena, an
-- atomic or a concurrent root without them, naming the missing declaration and
-- the file that supplies it (`crates/gabbro-cli/src/bau.rs`, `modulregel`).
-- *Never a silent default* -- which is the half of K7 that a measurement alone
-- could not have given: a runtime that quietly kept calling `vzalloc` would
-- have read as bound.
--
-- **WHY THERE IS NO `use` LINE.** Nothing in a program CALLS these items; the
-- runtime does. A `use linux::kmod::gabbro_kern_melden;` in the driver would
-- name an item the driver never mentions again -- ceremony that documents a
-- dependency the manifest already carries, and the kind of decoration this
-- folder rejects work for. What makes the declarations load-bearing instead is
-- the pair of checks around them: `gabbro build` refuses a module that lacks
-- them, and the C compiler holds each one against the generated driver's prototypes inside the
-- runtime's own translation unit (the emitted prototypes and the runtime's
-- declarations meet in `gabbro_kmodul.c`, so a wrong type is a build error and
-- not a wrong call).
--
-- ADDRESSES. A lock's storage crosses as a REGION (`ptr<normal, rw> u8` under a
-- constant clause `256 <= lenof(s)`: the generated driver's blob) since the
-- C-free lane's C2; the thread pair still passes the root's wrapper and its blob
-- as `u64` numbers, cast back in `linux-kmod.c` -- the one place a number still
-- becomes an address, and the next wall of this file (a Gabbro thread start is a
-- proved template on hosted Linux; the kernel's needs its own).
--
-- WHAT THE `costs` CLAUSES SAY, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT. Each one is the
-- primitive's OWN work -- the instructions between entry and return once it
-- runs. **Waiting is not in it**, and that is not a hole in these numbers but
-- the language's standing position: waiting bounds are NOT CLAIMED
-- (`grammatik/Grammatik/Zielsatz/Spec.lean`, the header's list), and a `lock`
-- declares the time it is HELD (`held <= N ops`) rather than the time it is
-- waited for. A binding whose acquire spun for a millisecond would break no
-- number below and no theorem above it; it would break the `held` clause of
-- whoever held it, and that is where the checker looks.
module linux::kmod {

-- **The ABI and the behaviour of all the bodies, as one named assumption.**
-- One and not one per body, because it is one fact about one file: `linux-kmod.c` is
-- compiled by the kernel's own build, with the kernel's own flags, against the
-- kernel whose headers are in the manifest's `kmod` line.
assume kern_bindung_abi
    "Every body in `bibliothek/linux-kmod/linux-kmod.c` keeps the x86_64 SysV convention of its declaration here, answers 0 exactly where the declaration says a refusal is 0, and neither sleeps nor faults in a context the module runtime calls it from."
    falsifier sonde_kern_bindung;

-- **The masking promise, named apart -- because a leg of the goal theorem rests
-- on it.** `masks irqs` at a `lock` declaration is a statement about the
-- environment: while the lock is held, no interrupt handler runs on this core.
-- `KernHaltE` (no same-core interrupt deadlock, `Zielsatz/Spec.lean`) is proved
-- over a model in which that holds, and the C side of it is exactly the pair of
-- bodies below. *So a binding whose `maskiert` pair did not mask would leave a
-- proved theorem standing over a program it is not about* -- which is why this
-- is its own assumption with its own name, and why the QEMU probe `takt`
-- measures it (`instrumente/pruefe-kernelmodul.sh`: a hardirq timer taking the
-- same lock, `landed=0` over a counted number of opportunities).
assume kern_bindung_maskiert
    "`gabbro_kern_sperre_nimm_maskiert` leaves no interrupt enabled on the calling core that could take the same lock, until the matching `gabbro_kern_sperre_gib_maskiert` restored what it masked."
    falsifier sonde_kern_maskiert;

-- -- the report channel ------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The runtime has no words of its own inside a kernel module, because printing
-- is a kernel call. A refusal travels as a CODE and two numbers
-- (`crates/gabbro-cli/src/treiber.rs`, `KMOD_MELDECODES`, lists the codes) and this binding's body
-- carries one sentence per code.
extern fn gabbro_kern_melden(code : u32, a : u64, b : u64)
    effects { pure } costs <= 512 ops;

-- -- the load verdict ------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The generated module driver refuses a load with the code of what went wrong
-- (the list in `crates/gabbro-cli/src/treiber.rs`, `KMOD_MELDECODES`); what the
-- kernel's loader is ANSWERED for it is this binding's word -- a negative errno
-- of the kernel it is written for.
extern fn gabbro_kern_verweigert(code : u32) -> i32
    effects { pure } costs <= 32 ops;

-- An arena needs nothing from the kernel since the C-free lane's C2: its storage
-- is a static pool of the manifest's `provision`, zeroed by the module loader
-- like all of the module's `.bss`.

-- -- the locks ---------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The generated driver owns the STORAGE (a zeroed 256-byte blob per `lock`)
-- and these are the OPERATIONS over it. The masked pair is a separate pair of NAMES and not a flag, so that a
-- program can bind a different primitive for the promise `masks irqs` makes --
-- and so that `kern_bindung_maskiert` above has something to be about.
extern fn gabbro_kern_sperre_init(s : ptr<normal, rw> u8)
    requires 256 <= lenof(s)
    effects { writes s } costs <= 512 ops;

extern fn gabbro_kern_sperre_nimm(s : ptr<normal, rw> u8)
    requires 256 <= lenof(s)
    effects { writes s } costs <= 512 ops;

extern fn gabbro_kern_sperre_gib(s : ptr<normal, rw> u8)
    requires 256 <= lenof(s)
    effects { writes s } costs <= 512 ops;

-- The masked pair hands the saved interrupt state OUT and takes it back IN: the
-- generated driver keeps it beside the lock, written and read by the holder only.
extern fn gabbro_kern_sperre_nimm_maskiert(s : ptr<normal, rw> u8) -> u64
    requires 256 <= lenof(s)
    effects { writes s } costs <= 512 ops;

extern fn gabbro_kern_sperre_gib_maskiert(s : ptr<normal, rw> u8, flaggen : u64)
    requires 256 <= lenof(s)
    effects { writes s } costs <= 512 ops;

-- Which core is running. The runtime records it as the lock's HOLDER, so that a
-- probe's own interrupt body can report whether it ever landed on a core that
-- was holding -- a green run that is a measurement and not an absence of bad
-- news.
extern fn gabbro_kern_kernnummer() -> u32
    effects { pure } costs <= 64 ops;

-- -- the roots as kernel threads --------------------------------------------
--
-- One thread per member of the unit's `concurrent` set. `start` takes the
-- runtime's blob and the ADDRESS OF THE ROOT'S WRAPPER and answers 0 or a
-- positive errno; `warte` returns once that wrapper has returned. A start that
-- FAILS must leave the blob in a state `warte` returns from at once -- the load
-- refusal in `kmodul.c` waits for every root it tried to start, and a blob that
-- never completed would hang `insmod` instead of refusing it.
extern fn gabbro_kern_faden_start(f : u64, koerper : u64) -> u32
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

extern fn gabbro_kern_faden_warte(f : u64)
    effects { pure } costs <= 100000 ops;

}
